 "lines": [
  {
   "text": "In 1943, the US Army strapped tiny firebombs to live bats.",
   "s": 0.12,
   "e": 4.141
  },
  {
   "text": "A thousand bats per bomb, each with napalm clipped to its chest.",
   "s": 4.441,
   "e": 7.542
  },
  {
   "text": "A dentist's idea: drop them on Japan, where they'd hide in attics.",
   "s": 7.841,
   "e": 11.038
  },
  {
   "text": "Somehow, President Roosevelt said yes.",
   "s": 11.338,
   "e": 13.143
  },
  {
   "text": "So in May 1943, they tested it at an airfield in New Mexico.",
   "s": 13.444,
   "e": 17.243
  },
  {
   "text": "Then it got worse: some armed bats escaped.",
   "s": 17.543,
   "e": 19.414
  },
  {
   "text": "They flew under a fuel tank, and hung upside down for a nap.",
   "s": 19.714,
   "e": 22.628
  },
  {
   "text": "Then the timers ran out.",
   "s": 22.928,
   "e": 23.868
  },
  {
   "text": "Whoosh. The bats burned down their own test range.",
   "s": 24.168,
   "e": 26.198
  },
  {
   "text": "So who did the bats firebomb first? America.",
   "s": 26.498,
   "e": 28.619
  }
 ],
